{
  "schemaVersion": "1.0",
  "scriptId": "pct-08-marks-and-aggregate-scores",
  "version": 1,
  "sourceType": "MANUAL",
  "entryNodeId": "start",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Marks, Passing Thresholds, and Aggregate Percentages",
    "description": "Solve examination problems involving marks, pass/fail margins, and weighted aggregate scores.",
    "teachingApproach": "Build the model step by step, explain why each operation matches the wording, show intermediate arithmetic, check units and magnitude, then practice transfer with specific remedial feedback.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 50,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal, Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 11 “Percentage,” printed pages 308–345. Approved scope: Solve pass/fail questions where marks are a percentage of a maximum, a candidate falls short of or exceeds a pass mark, or two candidates' results constrain the threshold. Compute aggregate percentage as total marks earned divided by total possible marks, not as an unweighted average of subject percentages when maximum marks differ. Include multi-paper credit and partial-progress problems from the chapter. Keep denominators and pass thresholds visible, distinguish percentage points from percent change, and check marks stay within possible totals."
  },
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Marks, Passing Thresholds, and Aggregate Percentages: Solve examination problems involving marks, pass/fail margins, and weighted aggregate scores. This lesson builds the method from its meaning, works through the reasoning and checks, then practices it with targeted feedback.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "A score percentage is marks earned divided by maximum possible marks, times 100. The denominator is the available marks, not the score itself.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_2"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_2": {
      "id": "teach_2",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "If passing requires p% of total M, passing marks are pM/100. A student who fails by d has earned pM/100−d; a student who exceeds by d has earned pM/100+d.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_3"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_3": {
      "id": "teach_3",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Two students’ pass/fail margins can determine both total marks and the passing score. Translate each sentence into an equation before eliminating the unknown maximum.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_4"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_4": {
      "id": "teach_4",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Aggregate percentage uses total earned marks over total possible marks. If subject maxima differ, add marks first; averaging subject percentages equally gives the wrong result.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_5"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_5": {
      "id": "teach_5",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Example: scores 90/100, 60/150, and 108/200 yield 258/450×100%≈57.33%. The unweighted average (90+40+54)/3≈61.33% is not the aggregate.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_6"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_6": {
      "id": "teach_6",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "If each paper has equal maximum marks, the mean of the paper percentages agrees with the aggregate. Equal weights are the special case that permits that shortcut.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_7"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_7": {
      "id": "teach_7",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "A pass/fail threshold is a percentage of a maximum. If a student gets 198 and fails by 36 at a 36% pass mark, passing marks are 234; thus maximum M satisfies .36M=234.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_8"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_8": {
      "id": "teach_8",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "When marks are expressed as percentages of different totals, convert them to marks before combining. Keep possible marks and earned marks in separate columns.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_9"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_9": {
      "id": "teach_9",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "For two subjects, “passed in both” is an overlap question. If data give fails in each and fails in both, count failed in either by inclusion-exclusion, then complement to find passed both.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_10"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_10": {
      "id": "teach_10",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "If the number of questions differs by section, aggregate by number correct over total questions. Weighted section rates must use each section’s question count as its weight.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_11"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_11": {
      "id": "teach_11",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Passing thresholds and percentage points are not interchangeable. Moving from 35% to 40% is five percentage points; relative to 35%, it is a 14.285…% increase.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_12"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_12": {
      "id": "teach_12",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "A candidate can fail by marks or by percentage. Convert the failure margin into a difference from the threshold, and only then solve for maximum marks.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_13"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_13": {
      "id": "teach_13",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Check bounds: earned marks cannot exceed maximum marks, and a pass percentage should lie between 0 and 100 for ordinary exam rules.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_14"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_14": {
      "id": "teach_14",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "For an aggregate with subject maxima, sum all obtained marks and all maximum marks independently. Then divide once. Do not divide each subject score and forget its weight.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_15"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_15": {
      "id": "teach_15",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "A final result should state the requested unit: marks, percentage, or maximum total. Re-read the question to avoid reporting the pass mark when it asks for aggregate maximum.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "focus_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "focus_1": {
      "id": "focus_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Apply the method to this new case. Before choosing, identify the comparison base and state the operation that matches the wording.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
